== Description == <!--T:1-->

<!--T:2-->
Profiling the code of FreeCAD helps find bottlenecks in the algorithms used to create or manipulate objects.

== Profiling a running FreeCAD session ==

The sampling profiler [https://github.com/benfred/py-spy py-spy] can record Python activity in a running FreeCAD process without adding profiling calls to the code. This works for built-in Python tools and addons.

# Install py-spy following its installation instructions.
# Open FreeCAD and prepare the operation to measure, stopping just before starting it.
# Find FreeCAD's process ID in your operating system's task manager.
# Run the following command in a terminal, replacing {{incode|12345}} with that process ID, then perform the operation in FreeCAD while the recording runs:

</translate>
{{Code|code=
py-spy record --pid 12345 --duration 15 --format speedscope --output freecad-profile.json
}}
<translate>

Open the output in [https://www.speedscope.app/ speedscope] to inspect the calls over time. To record until you stop it, omit {{incode|--duration 15}} and press {{KEY|Ctrl+C}} to finish. Attaching to another process may require additional operating-system permissions; see the [https://freecad.github.io/DevelopersHandbook/technical/Profiling-Python handbook's profiling guide] and py-spy's troubleshooting instructions.

== Profiling a section of Python code ==

<!--T:3-->
To profile [[Python|Python]] code use the standard {{incode|cProfile}} module to define start and end points to profile in the code.
</translate>
{{Code|code=
import cProfile
pr = cProfile.Profile()
pr.enable()

# --------------------------------------
# Lines of code that you want to profile
# --------------------------------------

pr.disable()
pr.dump_stats("/tmp/profile.cprof")
}}
<translate>

<!--T:4-->
Then install and use {{incode|pyprof2calltree}} to convert the profile output into cachegrind input.
</translate>
{{Code|code=
pyprof2calltree -i /tmp/profile.cprof -o /tmp/callgrind.out
}}
<translate>

<!--T:5-->
Then visualize this information with {{incode|kcachegrind}} for Linux or {{incode|qcachegrind}} for Windows.
</translate>
{{Code|code=
kcachegrind /tmp/callgrind.out
}}
<translate>

